WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.950
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan, and
in this video I'm revealing the top 60 Karate

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.710
Kid easter eggs, epic call-backs, and hidden
details you might have missed in the fourth

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.300
season of Cobra Kai!

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.809
Spoilers ahead, of course, so take care.

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:17.899
The name of the abandoned building where Johnny
trains his students this season is no coincidence:

00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:20.760
We meet out at the old Webber Industrial warehouse.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:25.200
Webber was actually the original surname for
Daniel's character in the Karate Kid movies,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.270
but when Ralph Macchio was cast in the role,
they changed the name to LaRusso to mirror

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.880
the actor's Italian ancestry.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.410
Johnny's reckless attitude to training his
students continues in Season 4 as they navigate

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:41.710
burning containers and piles of smashed glass,
leading Daniel to worry about getting sued

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.210
to which Johnny replies:
Uh, sue me for what?

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.950
This is a shout-out to Rocky V in which a
broke Rocky delivers the same line.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.822
Touch me and I'll sue.

00:00:50.822 --> 00:00:51.822
Sue me for what?

00:00:51.822 --> 00:00:55.720
And that movie was directed by John G. Avildsen
who also directed the Karate Kid trilogy.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.880
Although Silver is initially reluctant to
return to the world of karate, he eventually

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:04.390
takes out a hair band in the third episode
and pulls his hair back into a pony tail,

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.890
recreating his look in The Karate Kid.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.510
And the dramatic classical-style score that
accompanies this moment

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.730
is exactly the same theme used to introduce
Silver in the original movie

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:23.049
and it also plays as Silver relishes his victory
over Kreese in the Season 4 finale.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:27.560
The vanity plate on the car Silver lends Robby
for his prom night is a reference to the "Quick

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.090
Silver" three-rule method that the villain
explained to Daniel in the third Karate Kid

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.090
movie.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.090
[e8 32:18]
I call it "Quick Silver."

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.090
Rule Number One.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:36.090
A man can't stand, he can't fight.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.140
A man can't breathe, he can't fight.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.010
A man can't see, he can't fight.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:43.030
In Season 4, Silver teaches the first rule
to the students at Cobra Kai...

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.941
A man can't stand, he can't fight.
...and encourages Tory to use the third rule

00:01:46.941 --> 00:01:49.180
against Sam in the All Valley final.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.360
Give her a shot to the other eye.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:51.820
She can't see, she can't fight.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.130
Silver's third rule also comes up via the
80s martial arts movie Bloodsport that the

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.850
students watch at the drive-in.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:02.509
As Johnny preps Eagle Fang for the upcoming
confrontation with Cobra Kai, he warns them

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:05.490
that they'll probably play dirty, to which
Devon adds:

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.689
Like when Chong Li threw dirt into Van Damme's
eyes in Bloodsport.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:12.120
There's another Karate Kid 3 call-back when
Silver teaches the Cobra Kai teens about the

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.169
three things they need to win in a speech
which echoes almost word for word what he

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.739
told Daniel back in the 80s.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:19.760
There are three things that make a champion.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.939
The three Ds.

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:21.939
Desire.

00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:22.939
Devotion.

00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:23.939
And discipline.

00:02:23.939 --> 00:02:24.939
The first two, I can't give you.

00:02:24.939 --> 00:02:27.680
The last one I can, but you have to be
willing to receive it.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:28.790
Are you, Mr. LaRusso?

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.420
When Demetri does some digging into Silver's
past, he can't find much dirt on him except

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.059
for some details on a toxic waste scandal
in Borneo from the 80s, which is a reference

00:02:38.059 --> 00:02:39.480
to this moment from the third movie.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.209
What do you mean you can't dump it in Borneo?

00:02:41.209 --> 00:02:43.379
Who in Borneo knows what chloride sludge is?

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:44.379
Just do it!

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:48.430
Although Silver turns Kreese down on his first
visit, later when he goes to his wine cellar

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.840
he performs his signature wheel kick on a
bottle of wine.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.510
You're telegraphing that wheel kick.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:59.439
And that bottle's label, "Kamen Estate Wines",
is a sneaky easter egg to the original Karate

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:04.290
Kid creator and screenwriter Robert Mark Kamen,
who in real life has a vineyard and wine label

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.629
by that name.

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:10.040
Later on, Daniel tries out some of Johnny's
training techniques, one of which involves

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.560
doing push-ups on his knuckles, something
Johnny has already had Cobra Kai students

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.659
do and which he likely learnt from his old
sensei Kreese.

00:03:17.659 --> 00:03:19.659
Give me 60 push-ups on your knuckles.

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:23.849
When Sam and the Miyagi-do students refuse
to do the jump across two buildings Johnny

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:26.120
planned for their training, he gets annoyed
at her:

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.919
If you wanna sit in the back seat your whole
life, go right ahead.

00:03:28.919 --> 00:03:33.489
That dig is a play on how Sam was literally
sitting in the back seat when her friend crashed

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:36.809
into Johnny's car in the show's first season.

00:03:36.809 --> 00:03:40.799
During the Miyagi-do lesson about catching
koi fish with bare hands, Miguel falls into

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:41.799
the pond.

00:03:41.799 --> 00:03:43.829
Looks like you're a little wet behind the
ears.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:48.309
Daniel's reaction is a throw-back to the first
movie when he fell into a lake and Mr Miyagi

00:03:48.309 --> 00:03:51.319
said:
Daniel-San, you all wet behind ear!

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:55.760
Miguel falling in is also a little payback
of sorts for Sam who he mocked when she told

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.629
him about her Miyagi-do training.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:03:59.860
We train in junkyards and cement trucks!

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.729
And I've trained in that pond over there.

00:04:01.729 --> 00:04:02.729
Oh, a pond.

00:04:02.729 --> 00:04:05.510
And given how protective Daniel is about Chris
being sick in the boat...

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:06.510
Not the boat!

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.669
Not the boat!
...that's probably Mr Miyagi's original one

00:04:09.669 --> 00:04:13.700
As Daniel is thinking about why he needs to
fight Johnny again, there's a flashback to

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.850
the events of the first movie with a clip
from a deleted scene of young Johnny slipping

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.280
a pie underneath Daniel at school.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.940
This unused footage was teased all the way
back in the show's second season.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.330
He spilled a blueberry pie on my shirt.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:27.980
That's 'cause you put it on my chair first.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:32.660
The follow-up to that moment of Daniel getting
back at Johnny with the pie also plays during

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:36.450
Johnny's training montage as he prepares for
his fight with Daniel.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:41.660
By the way, Johnny's Badass Mixtape 4 that
plays in this montage is a little meta reference

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:46.350
as this is show's fourth season, similar to
how the second Guardians of the Galaxy movie

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.300
had a Volume 2 mixtape.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.890
And Survivor's song Burning Heart that Johnny
listens to while training is another Rocky

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:57.670
hat-tip as it was written for the fourth film
in Sylvester Stallone's franchise, and the

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:02.660
training montage itself, including the beach
scenes, is a tribute to Rocky's various training

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:03.790
scenes.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:08.160
Just before their rematch, Johnny asks Daniel,
Don't you ever think you might be wrong about

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.060
anything?'
which is likely a little nod to another deleted

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.190
Karate Kid scene which saw Johnny and Daniel
clash at a school drinking fountain, where

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:16.190
Daniel asked Johnny about Kreese.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.780
Well, you ever think he might be wrong, then?

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:22.330
Johnny asking Daniel why on earth he has a
rock in the middle of his dojo is a call-back

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.060
to the question Miguel asked Sam in Season
3.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.400
This place is crazy.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.400
What's the rock say?

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:28.400
My dad won't tell me.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:29.400
Or maybe he doesn't know.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.460
The inscription means "ENVY SEES ONLY THE
GARDEN WITHOUT THE ROCKS".

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.700
Which seems similar to the English idiom about
envy that 'the grass always looks greener

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.000
on the other side'.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.960
So it's likely a reminder to strive for internal
peace, focus, and balance, as Miyagi-do teaches,

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.950
rather than constantly comparing yourself
to others.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.990
When Daniel asks Johnny about his training
plan, he mentions some of the wildly unconventional

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:51.710
training techniques he's used before.

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.360
Some days I'll toss them into a cement mixer.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.580
Which is a nod to the second season when he
made his Cobra Kai students work out inside

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.310
a real cement truck.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:03.330
The showrunners have said they really care
about their episode titles, and often tie

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:07.050
them thematically to the episode's content
and to the original movies.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:11.670
And the eighth episode, Party Time, does both,
as not only does it feature the students'

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:15.930
prom and Stingray's house party, but it's
also a hat-tip to the moment in the third

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.610
movie where Silver and Kreese fight Mr Miyagi.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:19.610
Party time.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.700
And notice how Stingray, the person who says
those words in the series,

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.320
Party time!'

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.270
ends up getting his butt kicked by the end
of the episode, just as Silver and Kreese

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:32.130
were beaten by Mr Miyagi in the movie.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:37.500
When Stingray's neighbour accuses him of playing
kung fu with a bunch of kids, Ray firmly points

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.830
out:
It's not kung fu.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:40.080
It's Cobra Kai.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.300
That feels like a little wink towards the
2010 Karate Kid remake with Jaden Smith and

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.140
Jackie Chan which features kung fu rather
than karate.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.430
Interestingly: Jaden's dad Will Smith is an
Executive Producer on Cobra Kai because his

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:58.500
production company has owned the Karate Kid
franchise since that 2010 reboot.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:03.020
Daniel's cousin Vanessa is played by Ralph
Macchio's real-life daughter, Julia, which

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:08.150
adds to the humour of Daniel and Amanda acting
so sneeringly towards her offer as a child

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.880
psychology student to help them with their
son Anthony:

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.610
No offense, but if we were gonna get a pro,
we could do better than Marisa Tomei Junior.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:20.210
The way Vanessa confidently wipes the floor
with the LaRussos' condescending attitude

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:24.560
towards her based on nothing but their own
prejudice echoes how Marisa Tomei's character

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.270
did the same thing to a patronising lawyer
who refused to believe her automotive expertise

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:33.780
in the movie My Cousin Vinny, which also starred
the karate kid himself.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.930
When the tournament announcer introduces Miyagi-do,
he says

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:37.930
Give it up for Miyagi-Do Karate.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.460
They're ready to show the world that they're
the best around.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:44.620
That's a deep-cut reference to the original
All Valley from the first Karate Kid movie

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:48.810
and the song "You're The Best" which played
during the tournament montage as Daniel and

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:51.620
the other contenders fought through the qualifying
rounds.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:56.980
Fun fact, 'You're the Best' was actually written
for Rocky 3, but got the chop from that movie

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:59.070
in favour of Survivor's 'Eye of the Tiger'.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:03.960
And Rocky 3 gets name-dropped by Miguel this
season who mentions former rivals Apollo and

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.120
Rocky to persuade Johnny to join forces with
Danny against Cobra Kai.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.330
Johnny and Daniel's contrasting approaches
lead to some amusing takes on Mr Miyagi's

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:14.330
training techniques.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:18.800
So, when Daniel has the kids doing wax on,
wax off with his cars, Johnny has them cleaning

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.390
out the trash in his car instead.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:21.480
Get all the cans out of there.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.680
You find any loose change, it belongs to me.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.620
And when Daniel brings out Mr Miyagi's wood
sanding discs from the first movie for some

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:33.349
"Sand the floor" training, he discovers that
Johnny's ruined his plan as he's already smoothed

00:08:33.349 --> 00:08:36.449
down the whole deck with a machine.

00:08:36.449 --> 00:08:40.890
Johnny does gain some appreciation for Miyagi-do
techniques though when Daniel slices the top

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:45.589
off a glass beer bottle he's holding, a call-back
to how Mr Miyagi chopped the tops off four

00:08:45.589 --> 00:08:48.819
bottles of beer when some men refused to take
them off his truck.

00:08:48.819 --> 00:08:51.129
Don't forget it saved your ass a few weeks
ago from Kreese.

00:08:51.129 --> 00:08:52.550
And that wasn't the first time.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.009
That last line is a reference to how Mr Miyagi
saved a young Johnny from Kreese's choke hold

00:08:57.009 --> 00:08:59.060
in The Karate Kid 2.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:03.649
When Amanda refers to Johnny as "Captain Eagle
Claw", although she gets the name of his dojo

00:09:03.649 --> 00:09:07.480
wrong, it actually makes more sense than the
real name 'Eagle Fang':

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.839
Uh, eagles don't have fang-
The showrunners have said they came up with

00:09:10.839 --> 00:09:15.970
the "Fang" part of Johnny's dojo title because
"he thinks it sounds badass" and it's "consistent

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.130
with his ignorance".

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.439
And Johnny's misunderstanding of eagle anatomy
continues.

00:09:20.439 --> 00:09:21.720
When an eagle's hungry...

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.430
doesn't wait around for a fish to jump in
its feet.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:25.430
Talons.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:26.430
It's not talent.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.430
It's instinct.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:30.800
Johnny gets annoyed at Miguel spending time
with Daniel this season and it seems to especially

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.500
irritate him that they watched Top Gun together.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:37.800
Given Johnny's long-standing love of the movie
Iron Eagle, which was released the same year

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.310
as Top Gun and is similarly themed, I wouldn't
be surprised if he's also annoyed that Daniel

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:45.279
showed Miguel Top Gun instead of Iron Eagle!

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:50.149
Also, you can just tell from the way Johnny
describes Top Gun that he identifies personally

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:53.990
with Iceman's character and sees Daniel as
Cruise's "dweeby" Maverick:

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.170
Cruise is the worst in that.

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.200
Iceman, best of the best.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:58.850
In comes this new guy, total dweeb.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.029
Can't call yourself a maverick.

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:04.730
Something else that hits too close to home
for Johnny is when he finds out his students

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:08.279
tricked Cobra Kai into getting soaked by baseball
sprinklers.

00:10:08.279 --> 00:10:11.800
So, you picked a fight, you didn't show up,
and then you doused them with a hose?

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.600
That doubtless made him remember being on
the receiving end of Daniel's hose prank in

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.620
the school bathroom in the original movie.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:21.701
And there's a nod to what happened to young
Daniel after that scene when Anthony LaRusso

00:10:21.701 --> 00:10:27.009
and his friends bully a cosplaying Kenny then
chase him up to a wire fenced gate.

00:10:27.009 --> 00:10:30.180
However, unlike young Daniel, Kenny does manage
to escape.

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.200
The night before Daniel takes the Eagle Fang
students for training, Johnny warns Miguel...

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.690
Make sure defense doesn't turn you into a
cream puff.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:41.510
... which harks back to the insult directed
at Johnny in the original movie during his

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.269
tournament fight with Daniel.

00:10:43.269 --> 00:10:44.870
Johnny, you're a cream puff!

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.680
There was a subtle clue to Silver's wicked
schemes this season via the book "Leviathan"

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.199
that he's reading when Kreese arrives at the
dojo.

00:10:52.199 --> 00:10:56.100
The book was written during the Civil War
in England and argued that a strong ruler

00:10:56.100 --> 00:10:59.639
with absolute powers was essential for peace
and prosperity.

00:10:59.639 --> 00:11:04.240
This foreshadowed Silver's decision to get
rid of Kreese and take over as Cobra Kai's

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:08.970
kingpin, and also signals how the victory
over Miyagi-do and Eagle Fang cements Cobra

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:14.309
Kai and by extension Silver as the dominant
force in karate in the valley for Season 5.

00:11:14.309 --> 00:11:19.800
In mythology, Leviathan also refers to a terrible
monster that embodies chaos, is associated

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.839
with envy, and is a powerful enemy;
although one which is ultimately annihilated.

00:11:24.839 --> 00:11:29.009
All very intriguing foreshadowing for Silver's
role in the series.

00:11:29.009 --> 00:11:33.089
When Daniel teaches Miguel how to fix his
mum's broken car, there's a little Karate

00:11:33.089 --> 00:11:34.089
Kid throw-back.

00:11:34.089 --> 00:11:36.430
So you had to run after the car and jump in?

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.490
What happens if you missed?

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.881
That's a reference to the moment in the first
movie when Daniel was taking Ali out on a

00:11:40.881 --> 00:11:41.881
date and both he and his mum had to bump start
their car by pushing it along, then popping

00:11:41.881 --> 00:11:43.249
the clutch while jumping inside.

00:11:43.249 --> 00:11:46.970
Carrie Underwood's performance of "Moment
of Truth" at the tournament is a version of

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:51.140
the original song by Survivor which played
during The Karate Kid's end credits.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:55.139
And Daniel says it to Miguel this season after
they've finished fixing up his mum's car.

00:11:55.139 --> 00:11:56.929
It's time for the moment of truth.

00:11:56.929 --> 00:12:01.399
And when Daniel teaches Miguel how to drive,
they end up talking about music including

00:12:01.399 --> 00:12:06.559
the band Chicago, whose bassist and singer
Peter Cetera also gets a shout-out later:

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:08.009
Peter Cetera's badass.

00:12:08.009 --> 00:12:10.630
Peter Cetera is the opposite of badass.

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:15.420
Cetera's solo song 'Glory of Love' played
over the closing credits of The Karate Kid

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:19.720
2, and in another funny connection between
the Miyagi-verse and Sly Stallone, it was

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.279
originally written for Rocky 4!

00:12:22.279 --> 00:12:27.300
Robby's determination to win above all else,
to prove that he's not reliant on any particular

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:32.320
person or technique, comes undone by the end
of the season, proving true the Miyagism which

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.910
Daniel tried to teach him earlier on.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:37.290
Never put passion in front of principle.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.769
Because even if you win, you lose.

00:12:38.769 --> 00:12:43.759
That moment recalls the second movie where
Mr Miyagi explained to Daniel why he refused

00:12:43.759 --> 00:12:47.420
a fight to the death with Sato when he was
younger as it went against his principles

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:52.149
of not fighting unless absolutely necessary:
Never put passion before principle.

00:12:52.149 --> 00:12:54.600
Even if win, you lose.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.689
When Johnny goes on a drunken Twitter rant
about his upcoming fight with Daniel, there

00:12:58.689 --> 00:13:03.079
are some funny throw-backs like "hashbrown
dead meat", which is an amusing hat-tip to

00:13:03.079 --> 00:13:06.369
Season 2 when he recorded an online video
ad for his dojo.

00:13:06.369 --> 00:13:08.209
Put one of those hash browns at the end.

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:10.329
You know, like, "Hash brown, Team Cobra Kai"
or something.

00:13:10.329 --> 00:13:15.029
As for the "dead meat" threat, that's a reference
to when Johnny's Cobra Kai teammates threatened

00:13:15.029 --> 00:13:17.199
Daniel at the tournament in the first movie.

00:13:17.199 --> 00:13:18.459
You're dead meat.

00:13:18.459 --> 00:13:23.250
And it pops up again in Season 4 when Johnny's
trying to recruit Piper to Eagle Fang.

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:25.730
You're either a killer or you're dead meat.

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:29.420
Also on Johnny's Twitter bio is his Eagle
Fang's slogan "Bite First".

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:33.110
We bite first!
...which is his take on Cobra Kai's 'Strike

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.110
first' mantra.

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:39.059
And, amusingly, Johnny has had his motto,
'Bite like an Eagle', emblazoned on the back

00:13:39.059 --> 00:13:40.600
of his T-shirt!

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:44.689
And when Amanda refers to the upcoming fight
between the two old rivals as "a showdown

00:13:44.689 --> 00:13:49.069
at the Okinawa Corral",
it's both an allusion to The Karate Kid 2

00:13:49.069 --> 00:13:53.579
and Daniel's face-off with Chozen in Okinawa,
as well as a pun on the real-life event that

00:13:53.579 --> 00:13:58.459
was turned into a movie "Gunfight at the O.K.
Corral", a famous shootout in the American

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:00.860
Old West between lawmen and outlaws.

